  "Question": "For how many positive integers \\(n\\) is \\(\\dfrac{n}{30-n}\\) also a positive integer?",
  "Question Type": "text",
  "Choices": {
    "A": "\\(4\\)",
    "B": "\\(5\\)",
    "C": "\\(6\\)",
    "D": "\\(7\\)",
    "E": "\\(8\\)"
  },
  "Choices Type": "text",
  "Correct Answer": "D",
  "Solution": "Because \\(n\\) is positive, the quotient can be positive only when its denominator is positive. Thus \\(0<n<30\\). The value \\(n=30\\) is undefined, and larger values give a negative quotient.\n\nLet \\(d=30-n\\). Then \\(d\\) is an integer with \\(1\\le d<30\\), and\n\\[\n\\frac{n}{30-n}\n=\\frac{30-d}{d}\n=\\frac{30}{d}-1.\n\\]\nThis is an integer exactly when \\(d\\) divides 30. For every positive divisor \\(d<30\\), it is also positive.\n\nThe factor pairs of 30 are \\((1,30)\\), \\((2,15)\\), \\((3,10)\\), and \\((5,6)\\). Excluding 30 leaves the seven divisors\n\\[\n1,2,3,5,6,10,15.\n\\]\nEach gives exactly one value \\(n=30-d\\), and different divisors give different values of \\(n\\). Hence there are \\(\\boxed{7}\\) positive integers, choice \\(\\boxed{\\text{D}}\\).",
